{"indexedPages":15,"totalPages":15,"version":"2034","text":"Received 23 October 2026, accepted 28 October 2026, date of publication 2 November 2026, date of current version 10 November 2026.\nDigital Object Identifier 10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3749477\nTremorFusionNet: A Hybrid Deep Learning Model for Real-Time Tremor Detection Using CNN-LSTM-Attention on Embedded Edge AI Systems\nMARREDDY NAGA SABARI AND CH. DEEPAK , (Senior Member, IEEE)\nSchool of Electronics Engineering, VIT-AP University, Amaravati, Andhra Pradesh 522241, India\nCorresponding author: Ch. Deepak (deepak.ch@vitap.ac.in)\nThe Hardware Prototype Development Financially supported by VIT-AP University, Andhra Pradesh, India.\nABSTRACT Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a neurodegenerative disorder that begins with tremors and affects movements. These symptoms fluctuate in nature; hence, it is challenging to assess tremors in their early stages. Traditional methods suffer from high computational complexity, sensor noise, and inefficiencies in real-time detection, rendering them unsuitable for low-power edge devices. An advanced deep-learning architecture, TremorFusionNet, was introduced in this study. The model uses Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (Bi-LSTM), Gated Recurrent Units (GRU), and selfattention mechanisms to improve spatial feature extraction and temporal correlation, in the sense that it employs adaptive feature selection through self-attention and optimizes classification accuracy while reducing overfitting. To ensure real-time deployment on resource-constrained hardware, structured pruning and quantization-aware training were implemented, and the computational efficiency of the model was optimized. The proposed model uses NodeMCU for sensor data acquisition, Raspberry Pi 4 for data preprocessing, and the Jetson Nano for high-speed inference. The model is evaluated and validated on the ALAMEDA PD tremor dataset TremorFusionNet has achieved an accuracy of 99.52% and reduces the memory footprint of the model by 50% and consumes about 6.8W on Jetson Nano that executes inference in <8 ms, whereas cloud-based models require >1000 ms and 100W. Overall, the system provides a scalable and energy-efficient edge-AI solution for continuous PD tremor monitoring, and ensures real-time performance with minimal power consumption.\nINDEX TERMS Edge-AI, tremor detection, Parkinson’s disease, pruning, quantization-awareness.\nI. INTRODUCTION\nParkinson’s disease (PD), which affects more than 10 million people worldwide, is one of the most common neurodegenerative disorders. PD leads to symptoms such as bradykinesia, muscle rigidity, postural instability, and tremors due to neurological loss caused by the interruption of dopamine production[1]. Among these symptoms, tremors are the earliest and most noticeable. These tremors are vital biomarkers for early detection of PD. Advanced diagnostic tools and optimized treatment plans are required to facilitate timely intervention, optimize treatment plans, and improve the quality of life\nThe associate editor coordinating the review of this manuscript and\napproving it for publication was Xuebo Zhang .\nof patients with PD[2]. The Unified Parkinson’s Disease Rating Scale (UPDRS) is a standard clinical assessment tool. The UPDRS exhibits inconsistencies because it relies on subjective evaluations; hence, it is not suitable for realtime monitoring[3]. The characteristics of tremors, such as amplitude, frequency, and periodicity, vary throughout the day; hence, monitoring these changes continuously offers an ideal and real-time method to track the progression of PD. Neuroimaging techniques, such as Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) and single photon emission computed tomography, have been used to analyze brain structure and dopamine activity, in addition to clinical evaluations [4]. Although these techniques provide deeper insights into PD progression, they are time consuming, expensive, and\n171652\n2026 The Authors. This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 License. For more information, see https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ VOLUME 14, 2026\n\n\n M. N. Sabari, Ch. Deepak: TremorFusionNet: A Hybrid Deep Learning Model\ninfeasible for continuous tremor monitoring. In addition, these methods are performed at advanced stages of PD; hence, it is not possible to detect PD at early stages [5]. Other mechanisms, such as kinematic assessments using motion capture sensors, electromyography (EMG), and wearable sensors, have been explored to enhance the objectivity [6]. Infrared cameras and optical markers can be used to monitor hand tremors and movement abnormalities and provide highly accurate data. However, these systems are costly and require specialized laboratory setups [7]. Abnormal muscle activity can be detected using EMG based tremor detection tools. However, they are intrusive and unsuitable for long-term and real-time applications. With the advent of wearable sensors and Internet of Things (IoT) technology, there have been revolutionary changes in the ability to monitor tremors continuously [8], [9]. Devices such as accelerometers, gyroscopes, and electromyography (EMG) sensors can be used to capture fine-grained tremor patterns in real-world environments. They provide an objective and data-driven approach for tracking PD progression. Smart watches, wristbands, and other portable devices can be used to integrate these sensors to effectively capture patterns relevant to PD tremors. However, precision detection and classification require more sophisticated computational models to distinguish between essential and PD tremors. In addition, the use of these sensors presents many challenges owing to their high-frequency signals generated by them. Variable tremor intensities, noise, and motion artifacts, along with the computational limitations of embedded hardware, further worsen the development of robust tremor detection systems [10]. Transformative improvements in artificial intelligence (AI) and wearable technologies have enabled the development of machine learning and deep learning-based tremor detection and monitoring systems using sensor data. Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) are widely used for feature extraction, whereas Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) and Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) networks can capture temporal dependencies in sequential data. However, deep learning architectures are computationally inefficient hence, their deployment on resource-constrained embedded devices is limited. In addition, the CNN and LSTM models are prone to overfitting and require significant memory and power for deployment. Self-attention mechanisms are effective in enhancing feature learning; however, they introduce computational overhead, rendering them unsuitable for energyefficient applications. Therefore, there is a pressing need to develop a light weight yet high-accuracy deep learning model tailored for real-time tremor detection in embedded systems [11]. Most medical devices require seamless connectivity between devices, sensors, and cloud platforms to facilitate real-time patient monitoring and data-driven decision making. By combining smart sensors and edge computing, IoT enhances remote diagnostics, continuous health tracking, and predictive analytics for various medical conditions, including PD [12]. However, data transmission and cloud dependency\nare major challenges in IoT-based health monitoring systems. Early wearable PD devices used Bluetooth or Wi-Fi connectivity to send data to the cloud for processing. These methods suffer from latency and energy consumption and require a continuous Internet connection to provide centralized tremor analysis [13]. Because sensitive health data are transmitted and stored remotely, there are increasing data privacy concerns in cloud processing. Modern approaches are transforming towards edge computing to reduce latency and allow patients to receive feedback instantly without relying on cloud infrastructure. With edge computing, data processing is performed on embedded devices such as Raspberry Pi, NodeMCU, and Jetson Nano. These edge devices provide real-time detection of tremors. By integrating deep-learning models specific to embedded hardware, wearable PD detection systems can be realized with enhanced accuracy, efficiency, and power consumption. Traditional PD detection methods such as neurological assessments, neuroimaging, and motion capture are limited by subjectivity, cost, and scalability in real-time. The use of deep learning-based wearable monitoring for PD detection and continuous patient care allows early intervention. However, resource-limited embedded devices suffer from high inference latency, reduced real-time responsiveness, and high power consumption when handling complex neural networks [14]. By incorporating pruning and quantization, the models can be optimized for real-time implementation without compromising the accuracy. Other challenges in wearable tremor detection include low-power processing constraints, network bandwidth limitations, and adaptability to diverse patient populations. By employing structured pruning, weight pruning, and Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) pruning, the model successfully reduced the computational complexity without compromising classification accuracy. After applying pruning strategies, the proposed model achieved a 50% reduction in model size, 40% improvement in inference speed, and 35% lower power consumption, making it ideal for low-power wearable healthcare applications. The novelty of the proposed model lies in its ability to balance accuracy and efficiency, ensuring high-performance tremor detection, while being computationally feasible for edge deployment. The contributions of this study are as follows:\n1. A hybrid CNN-BiLSTM-GRU deep learning model with self-attention was developed to effectively classify Parkinson’s tremors while overcoming the limitations of traditional handcrafted feature-based approaches. 2. An optimized pruning pipeline that applies structured, weight, and SVD pruning to enhance model efficiency is introduced to enable real-time deployment on embedded hardware. 3. A comprehensive performance evaluation of TremorFusionNet in terms of accuracy, model size reduction, inference speed, and energy consumption is conducted to highlight its superiority. 4. Finally, the proposed approach was validated using the ALAMEDA Parkinson’s Disease Tremor Dataset\nVOLUME 14, 2026 171653\n\n\n M. N. Sabari, Ch. Deepak: TremorFusionNet: A Hybrid Deep Learning Model\n(https://zenodo.org/records/10782573?preview_file= ALAMEDA_PD_tremor_dataset.csv) to demonstrate its practical applicability of the proposed model in realworld scenarios. The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. SectionIIreviews related studies on deep-learning-based tremor detection and model optimization techniques. SectionIIIdetails the architecture of TremorFusionNet and explains the integration of the CNN, BiLSTM, GRU, and selfattention mechanisms. SectionIV presents the experimental setup, training methodology, and performance evaluation, along with the results, and compares the proposed model with the state-of-the-art approaches. Finally, Section V presents the conclusions of the study.\nII. EXISTING MODELS\nThe domains of PD monitoring and tremor detection have evolved significantly with the introduction of wearable technologies, deep learning, and edge computing. Integrating optimized and lightweight deep learning architecture models for edge deployment provides enhanced real-time tremor classification and prediction. These models address the limitations of the previous approaches [15], [16]. Supervised learning algorithms are used to analyze movement patterns derived continuously from wearable devices to detect PD, thereby enabling automated symptom assessment with a high classification accuracy. However, data variability and feature selection challenges require more adaptive and resource-efficient PD detection models [17]. Spatial and temporal gait parameters were explored for PD detection and classification. Gait dynamics are used to differentiate between healthy individuals and patients with PD as well as to classify severity [18]. However, variability in gait patterns and feature selection necessitate more robust and real-time gait assessment models. The identification of motor progression in PD using wearable sensors and machine learning employs continuous motion data collected from patients with PD to analyze PD progression trends and motor fluctuations. These models reduce the dependence on subjective clinical evaluations and mitigate challenges, such as data variability and sensor noise [19]. The sampling rates, sensor placement, and signal processing techniques also influence the reliability of PD detection. Data measurement characteristics have a significant impact on the accuracy of PD detection [20]. Therefore, high quality sensor data is essential for robust PD detection and monitoring. Supervised models use real-time sensor data to track tremor severity and frequency, without the need for constrained environments or clinical supervision. These models provide customized symptom management and reduce reliance on regular clinical assessments [21]. Biomedical signals and movements from patients were analyzed for PD detection and classification by exploring feature selection techniques. Classification techniques, such as support vector machines (SVM), Random Forest (RF), and neural networks, provide accurate detection. By integrating feature\nengineering, the challenges related to data imbalance and sensor noise can be mitigated [22]. Neural networks are highly effective in predicting disease progression and motor impairment. They are used to analyze motor symptoms and patient-reported data to enable personalized PD monitoring. The CNN and LSTM architectures reduce the dependence on subjective clinical assessments and provide real-time patient-specific PD management solutions [23]. Deep learning-based advanced feature extraction and classification methods can be used to assess the severity of PD with high accuracy. CNNs and recurrent neural networks (RNNs) can be used to analyze motor symptoms and tremor intensity [24]. An optimized real-time PD symptom monitoring device based on an interpretable deep-learning wearable system designed with attention mechanisms allows highly accurate PD classification with improved trust and usability. These systems provide robust performance against sensor noise, data variability, and real-time inference on resource-constrained devices [25]. Optimal data collection strategies, placement strategies, and signal types are key factors in determining significant data for deep learning-based symptom classification. Efficient data acquisition improves the model performance and reduces the computational overhead in wearable PD monitoring systems [26]. Home-based assessments, compared with traditional clinical evaluations, provide personal and unconstrained healthcare in real-time. Applying deep learning models addresses challenges, such as sensor noise, data variability, and real-time deployment on edge devices [27], [28]. The advent of cloud and edge processing has provided new avenues for real-time data processing and storage. Edge computing in healthcare monitoring allows real-time data processing of local devices. It reduces latency and bandwidth usage by minimizing dependence on cloud servers. However, cloud processing induces high latencies, increased network congestion, and privacy risks, and is less efficient for timesensitive applications. Edge-based efficient data collection frameworks for smart health monitoring systems minimize data transmission latency and provide optimized resource utilization in real-time. However, the energy efficiency and bandwidth utilization are major constraints in edge-based architectures [29]. By integrating deep neural networks with edge devices to process physiological sensor data, realtime prediction and analysis can be achieved with reduced latency, while ensuring faster decision-making. By incorporating optimized computational techniques, challenges, such as energy efficiency and bandwidth conditions, can be mitigated. Such optimized models allow continuous health monitoring with improved accuracy and response times [30]. Distributed data processing across local computing nodes reduces network congestion and improves real-time, largescale healthcare data analytics. The integration of deep learning algorithms provides optimal data storage, retrieval, and faster processing while ensuring faster and more efficient decision-making [31]. However, data transmission efficiency and system scalability remain concerns in high-performance\n171654 VOLUME 14, 2026\n\n\n M. N. Sabari, Ch. Deepak: TremorFusionNet: A Hybrid Deep Learning Model\nhealthcare applications. Multi-tier healthcare architectures integrate data communication, AI-driven data generation, and security use edge, fog, and cloud layers to provide real-time monitoring and decision-making. AI generative models are used for data augmentation and anomaly detection to improve the predictive accuracy of healthcare diagnostics[32]. The extreme edge computing framework leverages neuromorphic computing and low-power AI models to perform an on-device data analysis. These models reduce latency and cloud dependency, while providing dynamic adaptation to computational workloads to ensure optimum resource utilization and extended battery lifetime for low-power wearable devices[33]. AI in healthcare improves accuracy, reduces false alarms, and enhances decision-making in wearable realtime healthcare monitoring systems[34]. Deep-learning-based wearable device technology enhances diagnostic accuracy, optimizes data transmission, and supports personalized healthcare solutions. Robust edge device models balance performance, security, and real-time medical decision-making[35], [36]. Wavelet-Gaussian filters with Lagrange Neuron (WGF-LN) edge-driven intelligence frameworks for wearable devices enhance extraction and noise reduction to achieve higher classification accuracy and address challenges in real-time processing, energy efficiency, and scalability [37]. Edge-cloud-based wearable computing frameworks provide virtual rehabilitation and optimized real-time health monitoring with significant improvements in motion tracking, patient adherence, and adaptive therapy recommendations through sensor-based deep-learning models [38]. Studies have investigated Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs), Stacked Sparse Autoencoders (SSAEs), and CNNs for Parkinson’s disease detection using MRI, voice, and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) biomarkers. These approaches can detect Parkinson’s tremor by extracting relevant features from movement signals, identifying tremor patterns, and distinguishing abnormal movements from normal motor activity. CNNs can analyze tremor signal patterns, while autoencoder-based models can learn representative features for classification [39]. Table 1 provides a comprehensive comparison of the various models for Parkinson’s disease detection and monitoring. Despite significant advancements in deep learning for PD monitoring, existing models suffer from several practical challenges when deployed in wearable healthcare devices. The dependence on highly complex architectures, cloud-based processing, and the resource-limited nature of wearable devices impose limitations on the real-world implementation of wearable devices for PD detection and monitoring. Existing deep learning approaches have several limitations.\nA. High computational demand: Deep learning models such as CNNs and LSTMs with attention mechanisms require high computational power; hence, they are unsuitable for resource-limited edge devices such as Raspberry Pi, NodeMCU, and Jetson Nano. B. Latency Issues: Conventional models are based on cloud-centric processing and lead to higher latency in\nreal-time tremor detection, which is not ideal for critical healthcare applications that require immediate response.\nC. Limited Energy Efficiency: Energy efficiency is an important performance metric for battery-operated wearable devices and for continuous health monitoring in real-time applications. However, existing deep-learning architectures consume high power and are hence inefficient for such applications.\nD. Lack of model optimization for embedded devices: Most state-of-the-art models are optimized for highperformance GPU and server-based training; hence, deploying them on edge and IoT devices is difficult because of memory and storage limitations. E. Scalability issues: Traditional models do not effectively scale when deployed in large-scale IoT-based healthcare environments and hence lead to network congestion, prolonged response time, and reduced efficiency during the simultaneous monitoring of patients.\nF. Lack of robust pruning strategies: Few existing models use pruning techniques to reduce model complexity, which leads to inefficient model deployment on wearable and edge devices. The proposed TremorFusionNet model addresses these gaps using an optimized hybrid deep learning framework for detecting real-time PD in edge environments. TremorFusionNet incorporates model pruning techniques for efficient edge deployment. The following sections elaborate on the design aspects of TremorFusionNet, hardware integration strategies, and model evaluation process.\nIII. SYSTEM DESIGN\nThe proposed TremorFusionNet is designed for accurate tremor detection and monitoring of PD and uses a hybrid deep-learning architecture. The model uses a multi-tier architecture that integrates CNNs, Bidirectional LSTMs, GRUs, and self-attention mechanisms, as shown in Fig 1. This architecture provides spatial, temporal, and contextual dependencies extracted from raw sensor data and enhances the detection accuracy. Data acquisition can be performed using wearable sensors that continuously collect raw signals from the patient’s body. These acquired signals underwent de-noising and were normalization during the preprocessing stage. Real-time buffering is incorporated to ensure that streaming data are efficiently processed by edge devices. The preprocessed data were then fed to the feature extraction and encoding stage, where the CNN component extracted spatial patterns and effectively captured localized variations in tremor intensity. Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (Bi-LSTM) and GRUs were used to process the temporal dependencies in the sensor data and allow the model to recognize fine tremor progression patterns. The output from this phase was flattened and mapped to the decision scores. The flattened vector is fed to the fully connected layer using the ReLU activation function. This allows the model to learn the non-linear feature interactions. The final softmax layer converts the fully connected layer outputs into\nVOLUME 14, 2026 171655\n\n\n M. N. Sabari, Ch. Deepak: TremorFusionNet: A Hybrid Deep Learning Model\nTABLE 1. Comprehensive model comparison for parkinson’s disease detection and monitoring.\n171656 VOLUME 14, 2026\n\n\n M. N. Sabari, Ch. Deepak: TremorFusionNet: A Hybrid Deep Learning Model\nTABLE 1. (Continued.) Comprehensive model comparison for parkinson’s disease detection and monitoring.\nFIGURE 1. Architectural diagram of the TremorFusionNet.\nprobability distributions for all the classes. Listing 1 illustrates the training process for TremorFusionNet. The proposed TremorFusionNet model was tned using binary cross-entropy loss and an AdamW optimizer with an adaptive learning rate scheduler. This model was deployed on edge devices for continuous, real-time PD monitoring. Pruning and quantization-aware training ensures the model runs effectively on edge devices. The model uses minimal computational resources while maintaining high detection accuracy.\nWith the edge architecture, cloud-based computations are reduced; hence, the data transmission overhead is minimized. The final classification results are displayed to the patient and healthcare providers via a mobile application or a webbased dashboard. This system provides real-time alerts for abnormal tremor patterns, thereby enabling proactive clinical intervention. Let the raw sensor data be represented as\nD = {X1, X2, . . . ,XN } , Xi ∈ RT ×F (1)\nVOLUME 14, 2026 171657\n\n\n M. N. Sabari, Ch. Deepak: TremorFusionNet: A Hybrid Deep Learning Model\nLISTING 1. Training process of TremorFusionNet.\nwhere N is the number of samples, T the number of time steps, and F the number of features. A Butterworth filter H (f ) removes high-frequency noise.\nX\n′\ni = H (f ) ∗ Xi (2)\nwhere H (f ) is defined as\nH (f ) =\n1\n1 + (fc\n/\nf)n (3)\nwith cutoff frequency fc and filter order n. Min-max normalization ensures feature scaling.\nXnorm (t, f ) =\nX\n′ (t, f ) −min(X ′)\nmax (X ′) −min(X ′) (4)\nThe 1D convolution operation for feature extraction is given by\nZc\ni = σ (W c∗X c\ni + bc) (5)\nwhere W c and bc are the convolutional weights and biases, respectively; σ is the ReLU activation function and ∗ denotes the convolution. For a given kernel size k, the output is given by:\nOc =\n∑k\nj=1 W c\nj .X c\ni+j (6)\nUsing stacked CNN layers, the multi-layers convolution is given by\nZ (l) = σ (W (l) ∗ Z (l−1) + b(l)) (7)\nFor a CNN with L layers and kernel size k, the feature map width Wl is:\nWl = Wl−1−(k−1) (8)\nThe Bi-LSTM update equations are given by\nft = σ (Wf Xt + Uf ht−1 + bf ) (9)\nit = σ (WiXt + Uiht−1 + bi) (10)\nC ̃ t = tanh(WcXt + Ucht−1 + bc) (11)\nCt = ft ⊙Ct−1 + it ⊙ C ̃ t (12)\not = σ (WoXt + Uoht−1 + bo) (13)\nht = ot ⊙ tanh(Ct ) (14)\nFor bi-directional processing, the final hidden state is\nhbi\nt = hforward\nt + hbackward\nt (15)\nGRU update equations as follows.\nrt = σ (Wr Xt + Ur ht−1 +