, γ are sensitivity factors. The edge deployment accuracy degradation is given by:\nAedge = A − ηLavg (36)\nwhere η is the latency-induced decrease in accuracy. The coefficients α, β and γ represent the relative contributions of the dominant tremor frequency, tremor amplitude, and power spectral density, respectively, to the estimated tremorseverity score. The frequency component characterizes the oscillatory behavior of the tremor, the amplitude reflects the magnitude of involuntary movement, and the power spectral density (PSD) represents the concentration of tremor energy within the relevant frequency band. These three features were normalized to a common scale before combining. The coefficients were determined on the training set by minimizing the error between the estimated score and the available clinical MDS-UPDRS tremor annotations, subject to α, β and γ\n≥ 0 and α + β + γ = 1. Therefore, the coefficients provide interpretable relative weighting, rather than arbitrary empirical constants. In the hardware part of TremorFusionNet, NodeMCU, Raspberry Pi, and NVIDIA Jetson Nano were used to process the data at different levels. NodeMCU collects tremor data from the MPU6050 and ADXL345 motion sensors. These raw data were preprocessed and then transmitted to the Raspberry Pi edge node. Raspberry Pi 4 performs feature extraction and deep learning inference to provide continuous monitoring, making it a viable solution for wearable PD tremor assessment devices in real-world scenarios. TremorFusionNet integrates deep learning with edge computing for effective PD monitoring, with high energy efficiency and low latency. The model uses pruning and quantization-aware training to enhance computational efficiency and maintain high accuracy. The design can classify tremors and estimate Unified Parkinson’s Disease Rating Scale (UPDRS) scores from frequency-based signals acquired through wearable sensors interfaced with the NodeMCU microcontroller. The sensor network continuously acquires tremor frequency variations from a device attached to the patient’s body and transfers them to a cloud server. Processing in real-time takes place on edge devices, where the raw signals undergo preprocessing and feature extraction. Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) and Wavelet Transform were applied to analyze the frequency components and time-dependent variations. A band-pass filter(2)-10 Hz) was used to remove extra motion artifacts and sensor drifts. Features, such as tremor amplitude, dominant frequency, PSD, and other complex signal measures, were extracted for classification and UPDRS score estimation. Bi-LSTM layers were used to capture the long-term dependencies and tremor patterns from these features. The computational efficiency can be enhanced, and the signal representation can be refined using GRU layers. The ability of the model to differentiate Parkinson’s tremors from normal tremors can be enhanced using self-attention mechanisms, which assign different weights to important time steps in the tremor sequence. A fully connected layer and Softmax\nVOLUME 14, 2026 171659\n\n\n M. N. Sabari, Ch. Deepak: TremorFusionNet: A Hybrid Deep Learning Model\nactivation provided the final classification. The predefined classes in the proposed model included resting, action, postural, essential, and no tremors. The model calculates the UPDRS score once the tremor category is identified. For this purpose, the frequency, amplitude, and spectral power distributions are correlated. The score was derived as follows:\nSUPDRS = αf + βA + γ P (37)\nwhere f represents the dominant tremor frequency, A denotes amplitude, and A corresponds to the PSD. α, β, and γ are optimization parameters used in the supervised learning approach, TremorFusionNet is validated in terms of accuracy and scores using clinical benchmarks. To validate the score-estimation pipeline, TremorFusion Net-derived tremor severity scores were compared with the corresponding clinician-annotated MDS-UPDRS tremor ratings available in the ALAMEDA dataset. The estimated scores showed a strong positive association with the clinical ratings. This is evident from the Pearson correlation coefficient of 0.91 and Spearman rank correlation of (ρ = 0.89p < 0.001). The mean absolute error between the estimated and the clinical scores was 0.47 points. This indicates a close agreement between the sensor-derived severity estimate and manual clinical assessment. These results support the use of the proposed score-estimation stage as an objective replacement for the tremor severity assessment. Table 2 provides the mapping between tremor frequencies and the UPDRS scores.\nTABLE 2. Mapping tremor frequency to UPDRS score.\nIV. RESULTS\nTremorFusionNet was evaluated and tested rigorously in a high-end computational setup to assess its efficiency in the detection and classification of Parkinson’s tremors. The setup used for the implementation contained edge devices for real-time processing and a high-configuration computer system equipped with a GPU NVIDIA A100 Tensor Core, with 80GB HBM2e memory and 64 GB DDR4 ECC for parallelized deep learning computations. This multi-platform evaluation enables a comprehensive analysis of the model under different operational conditions and provides a better understanding of aspects such as computational efficiency, power consumption, inference speed, and memory footprint. Mixed precision floating point arithmetic is used to optimize GPU memory usage, and data augmentation techniques such\nas Gaussian noise injection and temporal shifting are used to improve model generalization. Real-time feasibility testing was carried out by deploying the proposed model on edge devices, such as Raspberry Pi and NVIDIA Jetson Nano. The edge deployment process also includes wireless communication modules for seamless data transmissions. The ALAMEDA Parkinson’s Disease Tremor Dataset was used to train and evaluate TremorFusionNet. This dataset was obtained from real patients with Parkinson’s enrolled in the ALAMEDA clinical study. It contains features extracted from raw tri-axial accelerometer signals acquired using wrist-worn GENEActiv smart bracelets during approximately 30-min in-clinic MDS-UPDRS assessments. The recordings were segmented into 20.48s windows with 50% overlap after tremor-specific band-pass filtering. Timeand frequency-domain features were extracted from each window. Associated tremor labels were derived from the corresponding MDS-UPDRS III clinical annotations. Therefore, the present study established a retrospective evaluation using wearable measurements acquired from clinically assessed patients with PD. No additional prospective patient recruitment was performed. Table3provide performance comparison between the edge devices, high-end computing systems and cloud-based processing systems. The performance of the system is analyzed in terms of latency, bandwidth, and power consumption. NodeMCU operates at ∼5 Kbps per sensor, while Raspberry Pi processes feature at ∼2 Kbps per device. Jetson Nano\nTABLE 3. Performance comparison of raspberry PI, Jetson Nano versus high-end computing.\n171660 VOLUME 14, 2026\n\n\n M. N. Sabari, Ch. Deepak: TremorFusionNet: A Hybrid Deep Learning Model\nFIGURE 2. Hardware prototype used in the proposed model.\nFIGURE 3. Training and validation performance of TremorFusionNet: accuracy and loss trends.\nprovides real-time inference with <10 ms latency at 5W-10W power consumption and hence can reduce the dependence on cloud computing significantly. The hardware prototype comprising of data acquisition node using NodeMCU and edge device using Raspberry Pi is shown in Fig 2(a) and Fig 2(b), respectively. Fig 3 illustrates the performance of TremorFusionNet in terms of the training accuracy and loss. The model rapidly converged within the first 10 epochs, achieved 99.52% accuracy with minimal deviation between the training and testing curves, and ensured strong generalization. The loss graph shows an efficient optimization process, as indicated by the sharp decline in the early epochs and stabilization closer to zero. The integration of CNN, Bi-LSTM, GRU, and self-attention mechanisms enhances computational\nefficiency without compromising accuracy. Table 4 presents the class wise performance of the proposed model. The model achieved near optimal values for all classes in terms of accuracy, precision recall and F1-score. Fig 4 shows the comparative analysis between the proposed model and other similar existing models in terms of precision, recall, and F1-Score. The proposed model demonstrated superior performance across all three metrics, achieving the highest scores in precision, recall, and F1-score, indicating its effectiveness in tremor classification. Compared to other wearable device models, the proposed model achieves a balance between accuracy and generalization. A higher recall signifies the ability to effectively detect tremors. A high F1-score confirms that the model is robust against fluctuations in sensors or other factors, and highlights\nVOLUME 14, 2026 171661\n\n\n M. N. Sabari, Ch. Deepak: TremorFusionNet: A Hybrid Deep Learning Model\nTABLE 4. CLASS-wise performance of the proposed TremorFusionNet.\nFIGURE 4. Comparative analysis of precision, recall, and F1-Score across different methods.\nthe efficiency of the model in real-world PD monitoring applications. Fig5illustrates the bandwidth utilization (%) and resource utilization (%) of the TremorFusionNet model as a function of the increasing number of connected devices. As more devices transmit data, the traffic in the network increases; hence, bandwidth utilization shows a steady increase, and higher data transfer rates are required. Resource utilization also demonstrated a similar graph. As computational demands\nFIGURE 5. Bandwidth and resource utilization analysis of the proposed TremorFusionNet model.\nincrease with the number of devices, there is a great demand for processing edge infrastructure. TremorFusionNet efficiently balances this trade-off, ensures optimized bandwidth allocation and resource utilization, and makes the model suitable for large-scale edge deployment in PD monitoring. Fig6provides a comprehensive analysis of the TremorFusionNet model in an edge-computing environment with varying numbers of edge nodes and connected devices. The increase in the latency as a function of the number of devices is shown in Fig. 6(a). This latency problem can be mitigated by accommodating a large number of edge nodes. Fig 6 (b) shows the bandwidth utilization of the proposed model. A larger number of edge nodes results in a more efficient bandwidth distribution. Fig 6(c) illustrates network utilization in the proposed model. With a small number of edge nodes, network congestion increases because of the latency involved in processing data from wearable devices. An increase in the number of edge nodes helps balance the network load. Fig 6(d) shows the processing time. With more edge nodes, the computational time is significantly reduced, as the burden on each node decreases. This improves real-time processing efficiency. These insights validate the ability of TremorFusionNet to handle large-scale edge deployment. Fig 7 shows the performance of TremorFusionNet against various existing models in terms of inference time. Owing to optimized pruning techniques, quantization-aware training, and an efficient hybrid deep learning architecture, TremorFusionNet achieved the lowest inference time. Thus, the model is well suited for real-time edge-based Parkinson’s disease monitoring and tremor detection. To assess the generalizability of the pruning behavior beyond ALAMEDA, pruning experiments were repeated on the Levodopa Response Study wearable dataset using the same preprocessing and pruning protocols. The results are summarized in Table 5. Because the label spaces of the two datasets are different, this evaluation is restricted to the common tremor-detection task. The external dataset contains wrist-worn accelerometer recordings from PD subjects along with clinical symptom assessments. This provides an independent test for pruning robustness.\nTABLE 5. Cross-dataset evaluation of pruning strategies on ALAMEDA and levodopa response study wearable data.\nWe observed a similar degradation pattern for both the datasets. Weight pruning produced the smallest accuracy reduction and SVD pruning produced the largest. Fine-tuning recovered most of the lost performance on both datasets.\n171662 VOLUME 14, 2026\n\n\n M. N. Sabari, Ch. Deepak: TremorFusionNet: A Hybrid Deep Learning Model\nFIGURE 6. Performance analysis of the proposed TremorFusionNet model under different edge node configurations.\nFIGURE 7. Inference time comparison of the proposed TremorFusionNet model with existing methods.\nThis indicates that the pruning behavior is not specific to ALAMEDA. Table6 illustrates the effects of different pruning strategies on the TremorFusionNet model. The proposed TremorFusionNet model was affected differently by different pruning strategies. This is because they remove the information at different structural levels. Weight pruning produces the\nTABLE 6. Effect of pruning on the TremorFusionNet model.\nsmallest performance loss, owing to the elimination of low-magnitude redundant parameters. Structured pruning completely removes neurons or channels. Hence, this causes a larger reduction in representational capacity. SVD pruning introduces the highest degradation because the low-rank approximation suppresses the weaker temporal components associated with the tremor patterns. Fine-tuning restores the lost feature representations to some extent. However they increased the test accuracy from 95.2% to 98.9% after SVD pruning. Fig8illustrates a heatmap of the energy consumption (W) of the proposed TremorFusionNet model as a function of the\nVOLUME 14, 2026 171663\n\n\n M. N. Sabari, Ch. Deepak: TremorFusionNet: A Hybrid Deep Learning Model\nFIGURE 8. Heatmap representation of energy consumption based on user nodes and edge nodes in the proposed TremorFusionNet model.\nincreasing number of user and edge nodes. The color gradient transitions from blue (low energy consumption) to red (high energy consumption) as power consumption increases with an increasing number of user nodes. Simultaneously, when the number of edge nodes increased, the energy consumption remained balanced. This demonstrates the ability of the model to efficiently distribute the computational loads. However, when the number of user nodes increases unequally to that of the edge nodes, the energy consumption increases significantly. Therefore, optimal edge node deployment is a critical step for real-time processing in Parkinson’s disease monitoring. The heatmap visualizes the power efficiency of TremorFusionNet as a function of the number of user and edge nodes. TremorFusionNet achieves higher power efficiency and reduces energy wastage per task, as shown in Fig9. As the number of user nodes increases power efficiency decreases because of the excessive computational demands on fewer edge devices.\nFIGURE 9. Heatmap representation of power efficiency (tasks/joule) as a function of user nodes and edge nodes in the proposed TremorFusionNet model.\nTo assess the practical deployment reliability, TremorFusionNet was additionally evaluated under continuous edge inference and controlled signal perturbations. Continuous inference was executed on the edge platform using streamed\nsensor windows. We monitored inference latency, memory utilization, device temperature, and prediction stability. Robustness was also evaluated by introducing Gaussian noise, temporal jitter, and amplitude perturbations. We simulated the motion artifacts in the test signals to derive their performance under adverse scenarios. The model maintains a stable inference behavior under continuous operation and shows only gradual degradation as the perturbation severity increases. This indicates that the pre-processing and temporal feature extraction stages provide resilience against moderate wearable sensor disturbances. Table 7 summarizes the results of the robustness evaluation. TremorFusionNet maintains high classification performance under common wearablesensor disturbances. Compared with the clean-signal accuracy of 99.52%, only limited degradation was observed under Gaussian noise, temporal jitter, amplitude perturbation, and motion artifacts. The lowest F1-score is 0.971. This indicates a stable performance under moderate signal corruption.\nTABLE 7. Robustness of TremorFusionNet under noise and motion artifacts.\nAs shown in Table 8, the ablation results show a progressive improvement when individual modules are added to TremorFusionNet. The CNN baseline achieved an accuracy of 96.84% and an F1-score of 0.963. When BiLSTM was included, the F1-score to 0.975. Addition of GRU increases the F1-score to 0.983. The complete CNN-BiLSTM-GRUattention architecture achieved the highest accuracy of 99.52% and an F1-score of 0.991. This highlights the complementary contribution of the four modules used in the proposed model.\nTABLE 8. Component-wise ablation analysis of the proposed model.\nAs shown in Table 9, the self-attention module increases the parameter count from 1.42 M to 1.53 M. The computational cost also increases from 4.31 to 4.80 GFLOPs. This corresponds to approximately 7.75% and 11.37% overhead, respectively. However, this additional cost improves the accuracy from 98.67% to 99.52% and the F1-score from 0.983 to 0.991. This indicates a favorable performance complexity trade-off for edge deployment.\n171664 VOLUME 14, 2026\n\n\n M. N. Sabari, Ch. Deepak: TremorFusionNet: A Hybrid Deep Learning Model\nTABLE 9. Computational cost analysis of different models.\nTo ensure a fair comparison, representative competing models were re-implemented and evaluated using the same ALAMEDA dataset partition, pre-processing pipeline, training protocol, and hardware configuration as TremorFusionNet. The results are summarized in Table 10. This controlled evaluation removes the variability caused by differences in datasets, pre-processing, and experimental platforms across previously published studies. Under uniform experimental conditions, TremorFusionNet achieved the highest accuracy and F1-score among all evaluated models.\nTABLE 10. Controlled performance comparison of TremorFusionNet and reimplemented baseline models under identical experimental settings.\nThe hardware evaluation used in the proposed model focuses on real-time inference feasibility and controlled robustness. The edge implementation showed stable lowlatency inference and robustness against representative signal perturbations. However, long-duration field testing under continuously varying motion, sensor displacement, and environmental interference is beyond the scope of this study. Prospective wearable device validation will be considered in future studies.\nV. CONCLUSION\nIn this paper, a highly efficient deep learning model, the TremorFusionNet model, is proposed for the classification of Parkinson’s tremors and estimation of UPDRS scores. This model integrates CNNs, Bi-LSTMs, GRUs, and self-attention mechanisms. By employing edge computing on Raspberry Pi and Jetson Nano, along with cloud storage for sensor data acquisition from the NodeMCU, the system ensures real-time tremor monitoring with minimal latency. By incorporating extensive pruning techniques, such as weight pruning, structured pruning, and singular value decomposition (SVD) pruning, the computational overhead is reduced while maintaining a high accuracy (99.52%). Quantization-aware training was employed to optimize the model performance for deployment on resource-constrained edge devices. By reducing the model size to 50%, the inference speed of the proposed model was improved by up to 38%. Increasing the number of edge nodes improves the performance and prevents bottle-neck.\nThe model reduces overall power consumption and provides superior bandwidth utilization, resource efficiency, and inference time. This makes the model ideal for real-world applications in continuous wearable-based Parkinson’s monitoring. In the future, the proposed model can be extended to include the optimization of cloud-edge synergy, integration of adaptive federated learning, and enhancement of multi-modal data fusion for more comprehensive neurological disorder assessment. TremorFusionNet is a significant step towards providing a scalable, low-power, and real-time Parkinson tremor detection system suitable for large-scale deployments in clinical and home-based monitoring environments.\nREFERENCES\n[1] Z. D. Zhou, L. X. Yi, D. Q. Wang, T. M. Lim, and E. K. Tan, ‘‘Role of dopamine in the pathophysiology of Parkinson’s disease,’’ Transl. Neurodegeneration, vol. 12, no. 1, p. 44, Sep. 2023, doi: 10.1186/s40035023-00378-6. [2] D. Weise, I. Claus, C. Dresel, E. Kalbe, I. Liepelt-Scarfone, S. Lorenzl, C. Redecker, and P. P. Urban, ‘‘Multidisciplinary care in Parkinson’s disease,’’ J. Neural Transmiss., vol. 131, no. 10, pp. 1217–1227, Oct. 2024, doi: 10.1007/s00702-024-02807-w. [3] G. AlMahadin, A. Lotfi, E. Zysk, F. L. Siena, M. M. Carthy, and P. Breedon, ‘‘Parkinson’s disease: Current assessment methods and wearable devices for evaluation of movement disorder motor symptoms–a patient and healthcare professional perspective,’’ BMC Neurol., vol. 20, no. 1, p. 419, Dec. 2020, doi: 10.1186/s12883-020-01996-7. [4] A. Rehman, T. Saba, M. Mujahid, F. S. Alamri, and N. ElHakim, ‘‘Parkinson’s disease detection using hybrid LSTM-GRU deep learning model,’’ Electronics, vol. 12, no. 13, p. 2856, Jun. 2023, doi: 10.3390/electronics12132856. [5] S. Shetty and Y. S. Rao, ‘‘SVM based machine learning approach to identify Parkinson’s disease using gait analysis,’’ in Proc. Int. Conf. Inventive Comput. Technol. (ICICT), vol. 2, Aug. 2016, pp. 1–5, doi: